Date: Sat, 18 Sep 93 15:29:46 PDT
From: thamilto@pcocd2.intel.com (Tony Hamilton - FES ERG~)
Subject: Using Bets to Incentivize Truth-Finding

Tim,

I am not _entirely_ against bets. Most of my response to you was written
in the context of "Perry Bets", not bets in general. As it has been pointed
out before, they should be of a reasonable size, should be founded upon
clear terms, and so on. Perry's bets simply do not qualify.

However, despite the fact that I do support _some_ forms of betting, I feel
it necessary to address some of your comments:

> - anyone can refuse the bet, but others will then make their own
> decisions about who stands by their words. Every school child knows
> how this works, what with bets, dares, double dares, and so on. "Put
> your money where your mouth is" is crude, but effective.

Here your argument is that betting is logical and valuable because school
children practice it? And yes, I was a school child, and I know how it
worked. Dares and double dares were the constructs of bullies. They were
almost always devised to convince another to do what the bully was unwilling
to. Crude, yes. Effective? For bullying, yes, but for logical argument, no.

> - it is possible that Perry, for example, is "bluffing." The stakes of
> his bets can be pretty high. But as with any bluff, if one is sure of
> one's facts, one can score big (sorry about all the "ones" in this
> sentence). In this case, if Jeff F. is as certain of his facts as he
> keeps claiming, then it's a great way to make a quick $1000.
> 
> - I don't know how many bets Perry has made, but my recollection is
> that bets have been offered several times since I joined the list last
> summer (July, '92). Never has anyone taken Perry up on these bets, to
> my knowledge. This says more about the others than it does about
> Perry, as the point above makes. 

No such point has been logically made. It certainly does not say more about
the others than it does Perry. You are interpreting the absence of response
as acceptance of Perry's arguments. This is completely illogical.

> Actually, it's quite noble. Richard Feynman urges that we always try to make
> an estimate of some quantity (a calculation, a physical magnitude,
> etc.) before seeing the answer--this exercises our estimation skills,
> gives us feedback on how to make better estimates in the future, and
> increases our confidence when we make pretty good estimates.

Let us see. Richard Feynman says it is so, therefore it is noble. First of
all, no matter what it _might_ be, it isn't so in Perry's case. Secondly,
estimations of some quantity have nothing to do with betting on existing
facts. Perry is not staking $1000 on a hunch that what he has asserted will
become true. 

> Betting is a way to do this. Small bets about future outcomes, or
> about something observable, are excellent ways to exercise this skill.

The problem is, the subject of Perry's bets are rarely "something observable"
in the simplest sense. This is the trap.

> Heinlein has some of his characters--I think it was in "Moon is a
> Harsh Mistress," notably--betting on nearly everything that comes
> along. They bet on the weather the next day (just kidding..they were
> on the moon), politics, etc.

But, of course, the bets are _reasonable_, something both parties can agree
on risking. Perry's bets are one-sided, and therefore uninteresting.

> My point: bets add piquancy. And as the street puts it: "Money talks,
> bullshit walks."

A very Platonistic statement if I ever heard one. It implies that the idea
backed by the most money is valid. It is also very short-sighted. It's why
big companies often pass up good ideas because there is no immediate "money"
in it, and is therefore perceieved as being "bullshit". A giant fallacy,
Tim. These cute sayings sure do sound fun, but they were invented by sheep.
These sayings are almost as bad as wive's tales and myths.

> If you ask the average American if he believes in ESP, he'll say he
> does. If, however, you put the question in the form of a bet, about
> some type of verification or convincing demonstration, his real common
> sense--paradoxically--takes over and he'll likely side with the
> skeptics.

Of course, that person can comfortably do so, just as Perry does, since ESP
would be a difficult thing to prove or disprove. But, by your logic, with
more people betting against it, and no-one taking the bet for it, it must
therefore not exist. You've allowed money to decide for you what reality is.

> Read Robin Hanson's article on idea futures and betting markets in a
> past issue of "Extropy" (maybe #8 or #9).

Futures markets are interesting, fun, and even valuable. But, the key here
is that eventually, we _will_ find out the truth at some point. Those who
were right make off good, and those who were wrong lose some dough. But when
there is only _one_ side to a bet, or only _one_ prediction, and the point
is never proven or disproven, it does _not_ imply that the single side of
the issue is correct. This is the trap of illogic you seem to be falling into.

> Bets are great ways to discover truth. No philosophical mumbo jumbo,
> just economic reality.

Bets are great ways to do just that, bet. Money does not equal truth. Money
can buy perceptions and fund research, but it does not create truth. Am I
alone in finding this logic completely invalid?

