Date: Sat, 18 Sep 93 16:18:27 PDT
From: thamilto@pcocd2.intel.com (Tony Hamilton - FES ERG~)
Subject: ECON: Some actual data!

Okay, I've been badgering anyone here for the last week who would claim that
the US is, in general, doing very well and getting better so far as the
economy goes. Some good figures have been thrown my way by those on the list,
but they didn't take into account imports, which is what I wanted to see.

Well, I did some browsing on the Dept. of Commerce service here on the
internet, and found some interesting figures. For the most part, you guys
are right. Things really _are_ getting better here. But, before I report
my findings, I'll just offer up the one bit of data which I've been trying
to stress. Exports vs. Imports.

In 1992, the US exported some $448 billion in "stuff" (the only thing the
figures said was that it excluded military exports. So, for those of you
who are claiming that service exports may not be counted, I really can't
say one way or another. If you know more about the nature of these govt.
figures, then let us know). In the same year, 1992, we imported $532 billion.

This is all I have been talking about. We buy a lot more than we sell. But,
alas, that is the _only_ problem I found in the "indicators". Everything
else looked just as everyone has been trying to tell me. This includes the
fact that the gap between imports and exports even seems to be declining
somewhat.

This is supported by the data on manufacturing. I can no longer claim that
we are manufacturing less, relative to the rest of the world. In fact, our
manufacturing production has consistently risen, while, for the last 4
years (the only data I could find), everyone else has been manufacturing
less. This includes Japan, Germany, France, the UK and Canada. And, just
as your other numbers showed, individual producitivity is also increasing.

Now, I'm not goint to spout out all the numbers (aside from those above),
since there were _way_ too many to look at. But, some other interesting 
things I found: We _are_ working more overtime than we used to. But, we're
also getting paid better (in manufacturing jobs, anyway). One interesting
thing was that the money supply has dropped by 4% in the last 3 years. Is
this a result of the import/export imbalance, or is the money supply number
irrelevant in the first place?

Well, anyway, it does concern me that it appears as though we are ciphening
large sums of money out of this country due to all the excessive importing
we do. It is encouraging that the trend is reversing, but is it fast
enough? What's more, if things really are as "OK" as they seem (and of
course we're just talking commercial business here, since we know the
govt. is in deep crapola with the debt and all), are they about to turn for
the worse with Clinton in office, trying to fix what may not be broken?

What does all this mean in the extropian equation? Well, from my perspective,
the better "we" do (businesses and invdiduals) and the worse the government
does, the closer we are to doing away with the government altogether.
Unfortunately, the world at large, in addition to our government, equates
the problems (debt) of a government with its people's problems. So, if the
govt. tries to pay off "the debt" with our money, won't that only work
to reverse this wonderful trend we've had going?


