Date: Sat, 18 Sep 93 17:08:14 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: META: Proposal for Paying for Advanced List Features

(I tried twice earlier today to post this and got bounced mail
messages. If my earlier versions somehow get through and you get three
copies of this, too bad.)

Harry has given us a nice summary of List finances--I'll need to print
it out so I can make notes and cross-correlate numbers.

My initial reaction is this:

* Very few people will contract for customized agents

* Very few people will "subscribe to" these customized agents

That is, the big project of the past calendar year (I heard you
talking about it at the January party in SF) is the "new list
software." *This* is the real value-added item, not the small add-in
agents, which may or may not get written (in the next couple of years,
which is the time horizon of interest to me, and probably many
others).

I can imagine some new agent paid for by some charitable person and
then subscribed to by a total of....10 people. (Many of us are barely
using the new list software to effect, so "Perry's Agent," which greps
for all mentions of foobarbaz and excludes them automatically, is not
likely to be "learned" by many.)

To put it simply, such fine-grained subscriptions, while perhaps
likely in the future, will "nickel and dime you to death." Just the
sending in of many different checks (absent digital cash) will be a
problem.

A possibility, of course, is to have some primitive form of digital
cash. This lessens the burden of transacting agent subscriptions by
paper. For example:

* I send a check for $100 to some account and receive 1000 credits.
(Forget the notion of using "thorns," for the obvious reason that
their value is not pegged to real money. I, for example, have
absolutely no thorns, Jay Prime Positive having appropriated my good
name and reputation!)

* This account is controllable through PGP-signed messages, just as if
I was contacting my banker and telling him to move funds around.

* I can do things like "Turn on Perry's agent for 30 days," etc.

* I can even pay for mail delivery this way--10,000 list messages for,
say, $20. (This sounds cheap, doesn't it. However, if the List is
putting out about 50 messages a day, then 20,000 messages a year are
going out. Thus, $40 a year would be required to see them all.)

(end of this section)

But I believe the easiest and simplest system is what I proposed
yesterday: charge a flat yearly rate of the "new" list software use.

The idea of giving away the "90 %" and then attempting to collect
nickels and dimes for specialized agents--that have yet to be
written!--seems misguided.

My BET: I will bet $10 that a "members only" new list software system
(with nonmembers getting access to the old software, or unfeatured
software, as I have discussed at length) will produce the following:

* The product of (number of members) x (fee for membership) > $2000

If my scheme is adopted, and this is satisfied within one year of the
announcement of the new plan (it takes time for conversion, after
all), then I win. If the scheme is adopted, and the revenue is less
than $2000, I lose.

Thus, I am betting that least 40 people will pay $50 a year for the
new list software, or 100 people will pay $20 a year for it. 

(This is over and above basic ExI membership. If ExI membership is
required as well, then some fraction of it needs to be counted in
above, e. g.  $10---the idea being that ExI membership would increase
somewhat, a benefit for ExI. The exact prorating of this amount could
complicate the settlement of the bet.)

The numbers above are outside my control, but I'm just betting that
Harry et. al. would set the rates in the "reasonable" range (around
$20 per year) and that then at least 100 people would choose to
subscribe to it.

Though I'm not prepared to set up a formal bet on it, I'll "wager"
that a new "Pledge Drive" begathon will bring in less money than it
did a year ago. And even less money next year, and so on.

The current "new" software is the thing worth selling. Or, even if the
new software had never been developed, requiring subscribers to be
members of the ExI is reasonable. I for one get a lot more out of the
List than I do out of the newsletter "Extropy."

-Tim May


