Date: Sat, 18 Sep 93 17:23:21 -0700
From: tribble@netcom.com (E. Dean Tribble)
Subject: LOGIC: Superrationality

	 > I think the distinction between Schelling points and superrationality
	 > is that Schelling points identify loci of cooperation ...
	 > and superrationalism identifies loci of competition

I disagree.  The big difference is that Schelling points are a
characterisation of observable patterns of behavior that help predict
human behavior, and 'superrationality' is a proposed strategy for
games that does not seem to predict human behavior.  This is not a
denigration of 'superrationality' (the quote marks around it are
sufficient for that :-).  There are lots of strategies that use the
concept of Schelling points to organize around, but Schelling points
are not a strategy by themselves.

Thus, Schelling points are netiher cooperative nor competitive.  They
are just the features of a terrain that are observable to the
participants.  These can be points to rendezvous at, or obvious high
ground to fight over.  Part of the underpinning of Schelling points
are common knowledge:  I know that you know that I know...(ad
infinitum) that the points are observable (less than infinitum is
insufficient). 

'Superrationality' in this context is that idea that there is a point
in the strategy space of most 'positive-sum' games that should be
obvious to all sufficiently rational people that is an optimal
strategy for all participants (and that it is obvious that it is
obvious, etc.) and that they will all therefore settle towards.  There
are several assumptions in this that I consider dubious :-)

	 And, as I'm getting sick of pointing out, the validity of SETI is
	 irrelevant.  The fact is that a number of SETI fans (including Drake)
	 came to the same conclusion (communicate on the 21cm band), from the
	 same premises (SETI is valid).  This makes it a real world example of
	 superrationality in action.

No.  It is claimed to be a Schelling point: obvious to all
participants, and obvious to all that it is obvious to all, etc.
'Superrationality' has nothing to do with it (SR is about competitive
games, not cooperative endeavors). Because it is a Shelling point for
frequency-based radio, several people in fact did find it.

But with the advent of non-sinusoidal radio, it has become obvious
that *any* frequency of radio is a badplace to rendezvous because
ou have to fight with all the radio noise in the galaxy.  Much more
sensible is to send a signal that correlates with some digital
signature (traitional radio sends a signal that correlates with a sine
wave). 

