Date: Sat, 18 Sep 93 17:23:24 -0700
From: tribble@netcom.com (E. Dean Tribble)
Subject: LOGIC: Superrationality

Kennaway says:
   The
	 assumption that there must be a correct strategy seems to be refuted, but
	 more than that I can't say.

That's the biggest problem with 'superrationality'.  It assumes that
there is a 'correct' strategy as opposed to several correct strategies
depending on ones circumstance.  Whether or not there is a correct
strategy for the non-iterated Prisoner's Dilemna is less important:
few real-world situations have so simple a payoff matrix, and so
simple an assumable state of the actors involved.  For instance,
what's the strategy become when one of the players can afford less
risk than the other?   How about when one of them can afford less
investment (so they don't waste the time screwing their head around
the problem), etc.

The diversity of goals is extremely important, since one of the
origins of this discussion was ethical systems.  It's interesting to
note that with real, smart people, in a real test of
'superrationality', they chose to *defect*.  All the rhetoric in the
world won't change that.

