get around to paying my ExI membership fee.

Now I would _not_ take this bet if he were talking about making
the whole list ExI membership mandatory.

In any case, I'm about to stop using the list software except for
rapid temporary unsubscribes when that becomes neccesary.
After looking at the list software, it seems to me to be something
that really isn't valuable to the single user, who can probably
use a mail filter of his own. (These exist in the public domain).

Which brings up what is to me a very good question: is this list software
supposed to be good for _me_ or good for the list as a whole (naturally
including me).

The one person that I know of that has excluded me said he made
that public accidentally, that there were no difficult personal
feelings, that I was just posting a large fraction of the
messages and he was trying to lower his received volume...

   (Why did I ask? I realize I need constructive criticism, and wanted to
   know what I did wrong this time...)

   (BTW: feel free to constructively criticize me. I've gotten two sorta-
   critical messages over the past month, but would appreciate some
   more specific stuff...)

Back to the main train of thought:

_BUT_: the main usage of exclusion in public seems to have been
       as a public and volatile form of chastisement. "You nasty
       so-and-so, you don't listen, you're unethical, and I suspect
       you're some sort of closet objectivist. 'I'd sentence you to
       hell...' but I can't, so into the exclude file with you!"

The vast majority of the people who have excluded publicly have done
so in such a manner as to imply that the exclusion represents an
ostracization by the vast majority of the list's members, and that
the average list member should shell out in the middle of reading
the exclusion message and exclude the offending user themselves.

I've used thread exclusion by title and found it slightly odd, in
that an excluded thread will sometimes stay excluded after a name
change and sometimes not, with no relation to the magnitude of the
name change (I suspect the software is looking at In Reply To: fields
that on some messages may not exist). Thread regexp exclusion seems even
more fraught with possibilities for mistakes.

A lot of those problems are definately more due to list members (including
myself) changing subject lines slightly, sending a response to the
list using "mail" instead of "reply" and thus losing thread identification,
et cetera, than due to any errors in conception or implementation
on the part of Ray Cromwell. We basically aren't writing subject lines
for the conveinence of the new software and the people using it.

I'm not sure, but it looks to me like prefix usage has actually gone
_down_.

Okay, people: comments?


